Yes. That is one of the cleaner institutional forms for what you were describing: guilds as durable professional organisms that sit between the individual worker, the customer, and the market.

Not medieval guilds copied literally, but modern guilds that combine apprenticeship, standards, pooled infrastructure, reputation, contracting, benefits, and bargaining power. They could work especially well for trades, crafts, independent technical work, creative work, repair, fabrication, construction, field services, and other professions where people are currently too atomized.

A modern version could have several functions at once:

* Training: apprenticeships, skill ladders, master-level certification, cross-training.
* Contract clearinghouse: customers post jobs; the guild scopes them, verifies competence, assembles crews, and handles contracting.
* Quality assurance: guild mark, inspection standards, warranty expectations, dispute resolution.
* Shared capital: expensive tools, shops, vehicles, fabrication equipment, insurance, software, storage, purchasing.
* Portable benefits: health coverage, retirement, disability, continuing education, perhaps childcare or leave funds where legally and financially viable.
* Reputation ledger: your craft history follows you independently of any one employer.
* Procurement power: buy lumber, steel, stone, textiles, equipment, software, insurance, etc. in bulk.
* Demand aggregation: instead of a lone craftsperson finding customers one at a time, the guild builds the customer pipeline.
* Crew formation: rapidly compose temporary teams around a contract, then dissolve them afterward.
* Succession: masters train replacements instead of their expertise disappearing when they retire.
* Emergency bench: when one member is injured, overloaded, deployed, or unavailable, another can carry the job without abandoning the customer.

The interesting part is that this creates a third mode between employee and isolated freelancer.

You can remain independent while belonging to an institution capable of behaving like a larger firm when scale is required.

Think:

individual autonomy + collective capability.

For contract work specifically, I would make the guild the trusted interface.

A homeowner, developer, hospital, municipality, restaurant, manufacturer, or government agency shouldn’t necessarily have to locate seven separate specialists and vet them individually. They could contract with the Richmond Building Guild, for example, and the guild internally assembles:

master carpenter + electrician + stoneworker + metal fabricator + designer + apprentice crew.

The customer buys an outcome. The guild coordinates the organism.

And you could have guilds of guilds.

